e dark:text-white sm:text-lg">&quot;<!-- -->Neon fundamentally accelerates our developer experience. It&#x27;s a huge reason we are able to ship faster without worrying about breaking things<!-- -->&quot;</blockquote><figcaption class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2.5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2.5"><div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-full"><img alt="Ben Vinegar" loading="lazy" width="32" height="32" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="pointer-events-none m-0 size-8 rounded-full object-cover sm:size-7" style="color:transparent" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fben-vinegar.10mtqyz0c9szg.jpg&amp;w=32&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fben-vinegar.10mtqyz0c9szg.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fben-vinegar.10mtqyz0c9szg.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/></div><div class="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-base leading-snug tracking-tight"><span class="font-medium text-black-pure dark:text-white">Ben Vinegar</span><span class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 dark:text-gray-new-70">—</span><cite class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 not-italic dark:text-gray-new-70">Co-founder at Modem</cite></div></div></figcaption></figure></section>
<h2 id="turn-postgres-around-from-bottleneck-to-accelerator" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Turn Postgres around: from bottleneck to accelerator</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#turn-postgres-around-from-bottleneck-to-accelerator" aria-label="Link to Turn Postgres around: from bottleneck to accelerator"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p><strong>Startup teams today are not hiring dedicated DBAs.</strong> Every engineer is busy building features and shipping quickly - they expect databases to adapt to their workflows, not the other way around.</p>
<p>Most managed Postgres offerings on the market do not fit this model - except <a class="" href="https://neon.com/">Neon</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>No DBA-level knowledge needed.</strong> Neon&#x27;s <a class="" href="https://neon.com/docs/introduction/architecture-overview">unique architecture</a> removes the manual work traditionally associated with running Postgres: no provisioning, no resizing, no capacity planning, no optimizing large instances, no manually scaling storage, and no worrying about backups or restores. Even small, fast-moving teams can afford to run many databases and stay focused on shipping software.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Branching workflows that match how you ship.</strong> Instead of <a class="" href="https://neon.com/branching/introduction">forcing all developers into expensive, heavy shared dev instances</a>, Neon lets you create <a class="" href="https://neon.com/branching/rethinking-the-database">ephemeral, lightweight branches</a> in seconds for development, testing, and CI/CD - just like you do with code. When you are done, you (or a script, automation, or API call) delete them. All environments stay in sync with production without manual work or coordination.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="neon-does-the-boring-dba-work-so-you-do-not-have-to" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Neon does the boring DBA work so you do not have to</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#neon-does-the-boring-dba-work-so-you-do-not-have-to" aria-label="Link to Neon does the boring DBA work so you do not have to"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Neon autoscaling adjusts compute based on workload demand" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fautoscaling-based-on-demand.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fautoscaling-based-on-demand.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fautoscaling-based-on-demand.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
<p>Neon&#x27;s serverless architecture takes care of the database tasks that typically eat up time on small teams:</p>
<h3 id="automatic-provisioning-vs-manual-capacity-planning" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>Automatic provisioning vs. manual capacity planning</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#automatic-provisioning-vs-manual-capacity-planning" aria-label="Link to Automatic provisioning vs. manual capacity planning"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>With Neon, there is no need to provision instances, size disks, or plan for future growth. Compute and storage are managed automatically. You do not have to think about which instance size you need today or how you will scale it six months from now.</p>
<h3 id="autoscaling-vs-overprovisioning-for-just-in-case-traffic" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>Autoscaling vs. overprovisioning for &quot;just in case&quot; traffic</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#autoscaling-vs-overprovisioning-for-just-in-case-traffic" aria-label="Link to Autoscaling vs. overprovisioning for &quot;just in case&quot; traffic"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>Instead of forcing you to overprovision compute instances to cover sporadic traffic spikes, <a class="" href="https://neon.com/docs/introduction/autoscaling">Neon automatically scales compute between a minimum and maximum based on demand</a>. You get protection against unexpectedly high load without manually resizing databases or pre-provisioning excess capacity, and you only pay when your database actually needs it. When demand drops, compute scales back down quickly.</p>
<div class="admonition not-prose my-9 rounded-none border-l-2 bg-gray-new-98 py-4 pr-5 pl-[1.125rem] dark:bg-gray-new-8 border-[#BE8A3C] dark:border-[#FFED9C] [&amp;_pre]:px-4 [&amp;_pre]:py-3 [&amp;_pre_code]:text-sm! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:bg-white! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:dark:bg-gray-new-8!"><div class="flex items-center gap-1 text-[#BE8A3C] dark:text-[#FFED9C]"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 16 16" class="shrink-0"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-miterlimit="10" d="M8 11.5a5 5 0 1 0 0-10 5 5 0 0 0 0 10Z"></path><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-miterlimit="10" d="M5 6.5a3 3 0 0 1 3-3"></path><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-miterlimit="10" d="m5.781 11 .282 3H10l.281-3"></path><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-miterlimit="10" d="M7 15h2"></path></svg><h4 class="font-mono text-[13px] leading-none font-medium -tracking-snug uppercase">tip</h4></div><div class="admonition-text mt-2.5 text-base leading-normal tracking-extra-tight text-gray-new-20 dark:text-gray-new-85 [&amp;_a]:rounded-sm [&amp;_li]:text-base [&amp;_li]:leading-snug [&amp;_li]:tracking-extra-tight [&amp;_ul]:my-0 [&amp;_ol]:my-0"><p>Read our <a class="" href="https://neon.com/autoscaling-report">Autoscaling Report</a> for data on the effects of Neon&#x27;s autoscaling across thousands of production workloads.</p></div></div>
<h3 id="scale-to-zero-vs-paying-for-inactive-environments" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>Scale to zero vs. paying for inactive environments</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#scale-to-zero-vs-paying-for-inactive-environments" aria-label="Link to Scale to zero vs. paying for inactive environments"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>Development, preview, and test databases do not need to run 24/7 - and you should not be paying for them either. In Neon, <a class="" href="https://neon.com/docs/introduction/scale-to-zero">non-production databases scale to zero when idle</a>. Teams do not pay for environments that are not actively in use, and they do not need to manually pause, resume, or clean them up.</p>
<h3 id="apis-and-automation-vs-manual-database-ops" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>APIs and automation vs. manual database ops</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#apis-and-automation-vs-manual-database-ops" aria-label="Link to APIs and automation vs. manual database ops"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>All of this is exposed through a simple, intuitive API that works with the tools developers already use. Neon is compatible with all major frameworks and ORMs, and because it is still Postgres, there is no new database model to learn.</p>
<h2 id="remove-friction-with-branching-workflows" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Remove friction with branching workflows</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#remove-friction-with-branching-workflows" aria-label="Link to Remove friction with branching workflows"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<section class="quote my-8 border-l-2 border-green-44 pl-6"><figure class="flex flex-col gap-5"><blockquote class="max-w-[710px] border-none p-0 font-mono text-xl leading-snug font-normal tracking-extra-tight text-black-pure dark:text-white sm:text-lg">&quot;<!-- -->With Neon, we found a way to scale our setup more efficiently, using branching instead of duplicating instances and autoscaling to match our actual load.<!-- -->&quot;</blockquote><figcaption class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2.5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2.5"><div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-full"><img alt="Thorsten Rieß" loading="lazy" width="32" height="32" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="pointer-events-none m-0 size-8 rounded-full object-cover sm:size-7" style="color:transparent" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fthorsten-riess.04jq_dcsthg2w.jpg&amp;w=32&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fthorsten-riess.04jq_dcsthg2w.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fthorsten-riess.04jq_dcsthg2w.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/></div><div class="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-base leading-snug tracking-tight"><span class="font-medium text-black-pure dark:text-white">Thorsten Rieß</span><span class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 dark:text-gray-new-70">—</span><cite class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 not-italic dark:text-gray-new-70">Software Architect at traconiq</cite></div></div></figcaption></figure></section>
<p>If Neon&#x27;s serverless architecture is the foundation that enables speed, Neon branches are the magic trick that takes it to the next level. Built on Neon&#x27;s copy-on-write architecture, branches are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Created instantly</strong> - no matter how much data you have</li>
<li><strong>Exact copies of production</strong> - schema and data</li>
<li><strong>Fully API-friendly</strong> - built for automation</li>
<li><strong>Affordable by default</strong> - they do not duplicate storage and do not consume compute while inactive</li>
</ul>
<p>Branches turn the database into a resource you can create, use, and throw away as part of your normal development flow.</p>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Neon branching workflow for isolated environments" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fneon-branching-workflow-overview.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fneon-branching-workflow-overview.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fneon-branching-workflow-overview.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
<h3 id="using-branches-as-environments" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>Using branches as environments</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#using-branches-as-environments" aria-label="Link to Using branches as environments"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>In traditional managed Postgres setups, you&#x27;d be running separate database instances for staging, dev, and testing, then manually configuring them and constantly trying to keep them &quot;close enough&quot; to production. That work never really ends.</p>
<p>In Neon, a new branch is an isolated environment - one that already contains your full production history, schema, and configuration. You start from production, deploy a branch instantly, and move on.</p>
<section class="quote my-8 border-l-2 border-green-44 pl-6"><figure class="flex flex-col gap-5"><blockquote class="max-w-[710px] border-none p-0 font-mono text-xl leading-snug font-normal tracking-extra-tight text-black-pure dark:text-white sm:text-lg">&quot;<!-- -->The services that touched schema changes or write-heavy paths could never share a database safely. Now every sandbox gets its own isolated Postgres DB whenever required<!-- -->&quot;</blockquote><figcaption class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2.5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2.5"><div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-full"><img alt="Joe Horsnell" loading="lazy" width="32" height="32" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="pointer-events-none m-0 size-8 rounded-full object-cover sm:size-7" style="color:transparent" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjoe-horsnell.024le1qrcp7h9.jpg&amp;w=32&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjoe-horsnell.024le1qrcp7h9.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjoe-horsnell.024le1qrcp7h9.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/></div><div class="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-base leading-snug tracking-tight"><span class="font-medium text-black-pure dark:text-white">Joe Horsnell</span><span class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 dark:text-gray-new-70">—</span><cite class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 not-italic dark:text-gray-new-70">Principal Platform Engineer at Bitso</cite></div></div></figcaption></figure></section>
<h3 id="staging-that-resyncs-with-production-in-one-api-call" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>Staging that resyncs with production in one API call</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#staging-that-resyncs-with-production-in-one-api-call" aria-label="Link to Staging that resyncs with production in one API call"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>In Neon, staging is just a branch derived from production. When production changes, you do not need to rebuild staging from scratch or run complex sync jobs. You can reset your staging branch from production in a single API call, instantly bringing schema and data back in sync.</p>
<p>This makes staging a reliable checkpoint instead of a slowly drifting approximation, and removes a whole class of &quot;works in staging but not in prod&quot; issues.</p>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Reset staging from production with a single API call" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fstaging-resync-from-production-api.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fstaging-resync-from-production-api.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fstaging-resync-from-production-api.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
<p><a class="" href="https://neon.com/branching/production-staging-workflows">Read more about using branches for staging</a></p>
<h3 id="dev-environments-for-every-developer-pr-or-experiment" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>Dev environments for every developer, PR, or experiment</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#dev-environments-for-every-developer-pr-or-experiment" aria-label="Link to Dev environments for every developer, PR, or experiment"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>Branches make it practical to give every developer - and every PR - its own database environment. Each environment is isolated, production-like, and safe to break. Developers can test migrations, schema changes, and data-heavy features without coordinating with each other or worrying about corrupting shared state.</p>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Per-developer and per-PR isolated database branches" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fper-developer-and-pr-database-branches.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fper-developer-and-pr-database-branches.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Fper-developer-and-pr-database-branches.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
<p><a class="" href="https://neon.com/branching/branching-workflows-for-development">Read more about using branches for development</a></p>
<h3 id="promote-from-dev-to-prod-safely-even-with-many-parallel-environments" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>Promote from dev to prod safely, even with many parallel environments</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#promote-from-dev-to-prod-safely-even-with-many-parallel-environments" aria-label="Link to Promote from dev to prod safely, even with many parallel environments"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>As teams scale, promotion becomes harder. Multiple developers, multiple branches, multiple schema changes - all converging on production.</p>
<p>Neon supports promotion workflows built on branching and snapshots. Teams can validate changes in isolated branches, promote them intentionally, and keep a rollback point ready in case something goes wrong.</p>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Promote validated changes from development to production safely" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Finstant-recovery-with-branches-and-snapshots.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Finstant-recovery-with-branches-and-snapshots.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Ffast-dev-workflows%2Finstant-recovery-with-branches-and-snapshots.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
